==Trivia==
*The "rowley" in Owlbrowley may be a reference to [[wikipedia:Aleister Crowley|Aleister Crowley]], an English occultist and mystic dubbed "the wickedest man in the world". In this context, the abyss in their description may refer to the [[wikipedia:Abyss (Thelema)|Abyss]] from Crowley's religion [[wikipedia:Thelema|Thelema]].
*Since [[Diabrollow]] and [[Sage of Life Dr. Nova]] appear to be based on Greek mythology, Prof. Owlbrowley may represent Hades' orchardist, [[wikipedia:Ascalaphus (son of Acheron)|Ascalaphus]], whose name means "screech-owl". After Persephone was granted permission to leave the Underworld, provided she had not eaten anything, Ascalaphus informed the other gods that she had eaten pomegranate seeds there, forcing her to spend part of the year in the Underworld as Hades' wife. As punishment, Ascalaphus was pinned under a stone in the Underworld by either Demeter or Persephone until Heracles freed him, causing the goddess to turn him into a screech-owl, a sacred bird of Hades and herald of ill omen described by [[wikipedia:Ovid|Ovid]] as "the vilest bird; a messenger of grief; the lazy owl; sad omen to mankind." This is vaguely similar to how Prof. Owlbrowley put a [[wikipedia:Mortarboard|mortarboard]] with a [[:Category:Sage Enemies|Sage]] stone on their head, giving them knowledge of the underworld and turning them into a much more dishonorable professor who leads their students to doom. Their [[:Category:Dark Enemies|Dark]] trait may reference how Ascalaphus is literally the son of darkness—the name of his nymph mother, [[wikipedia:Orphne|Orphne]], means "darkness".
**As a wise Sage and professor, Owlbrowley could also represent the [[wikipedia:Owl of Athena|Owl of Athena]], a symbol of the goddess of wisdom and war. In contrast to her more violent brother Ares, Athena represents the tactics, strategy and intellectual side of war, which is somewhat shown in Prof. Owlbrowley's skill set compared to [[Owlbrow]]: instead of just dealing damage, they freeze units they hit, but have an uptime just short enough for those knocked back to re-enter their range and potentially get hit again.
*Prof. Owlbrowley's description and Sage type may allude to [[wikia:mythus:Stolas|Stolas]], a wise Great Prince of Hell from the ''[[wikipedia:Ars Goetia|Ars Goetia]]'' who is typically depicted as a owl and teaches knowledge of precious stones, as well as astronomy and herbs.
*Prof. Owlbrowley's Dark trait may be a pun on how the Japanese word ''fukurō'' (owl) contains ''kuro'' (black). Prof. Owlbrowley's Japanese name contains ''fukurō'', but other Owlbrow variants do not.
*Prof. Owlbrowley's eye color may have been inspired by the [[wikipedia:Japanese scops owl|Japanese scops owl]], which also has red eyes, while their beard may reference the [[wikipedia:Bearded screech owl|bearded screech owl]].
*Prof. Owlbrowley is tied with [[Johnnyleon]] and [[Lophiiformes Angaburu]] for the most types of any non-event enemy, with three.
*Prof. Owlbrowley is the first Sage enemy to have multiple traits, those being Dark and [[:Category:Floating Enemies|Floating]].

